## 11. Images, audio, and educational videos

### Visual learning

Support illustrations, diagrams, counting objects, number lines, shapes, and other visual aids.

Use visuals to teach the concept, not just decorate the page.

Provide text alternatives and accessible labels.

### Text-to-speech

Allow lesson instructions and explanations to be read aloud.

Provide simple play, pause, and replay controls. Store voice preferences where appropriate.

Do not play audio automatically in a way that surprises the child.

### Speech input

Treat voice-based answers as an enhanced feature.

Handle recognition errors, background noise, accents, and language selection. Give children a way to correct recognized text or switch to typing or tapping.

Do not claim to assess pronunciation accurately until the chosen speech service and assessment logic have been tested.

### Video recommendations

Recommend selected educational videos that are relevant to the current concept and age group.

Use a curated catalogue or vetted search results. Store metadata such as title, provider, language, subject, age suitability, and review status.

Do not provide unrestricted video browsing to young children. Avoid autoplay and distracting recommendations.

Videos should supplement the tutor, not replace the lesson or become an uncontrolled path away from learning.

